hing_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Each input has a few horizontal lines (rows of colour that run from one edge or meet other lines). Every line has single-cell **gaps** of background. At the bottom edge there are short vertical **seeds**, one per gap column:\n- Pair 1: seed 1 in column 3 and seed 5 in column 9.\n- Pair 2: seed 3 in column 3 and seed 4 in column 9.\n- Pair 3: seed 1 at the bottom of columns 1 and 9.\n\nAll gaps in the horizontal lines sit in exactly those seed columns.\n\n**What the output does.** Each seed column becomes a full vertical line, from the top of the grid down to the seed. The vertical line changes colour as it passes each horizontal line:\n- Any cell above a horizontal line, up to the previous line, takes the colour of that line.\n- The gap cell itself also takes that line's colour.\n- Cells below the last line take the seed's colour.\n\nEvidence from pair 1, column 3:\n- Rows 0–3 become 3, because the 3-line is at row 3.\n- Rows 4–8 become 4, because the 4-line is at row 8.\n- Rows 9–10 become 1, the seed colour.\n\nColumn 9 works the same way: rows 0–6 become 6, rows 7–8 become 7, and the seed 5 is already there.\n\nSome lines change colour partway along, like `4 4 4 0 4 4 4 7 7 0 7 7…`. In those cases the colour used at each gap is the colour of that gap's immediate neighbours: 4 at column 3 and 7 at column 9. Pair 2's row 9 (`…2 2 3 0 3 3…`) gives 3 at column 9, as the output shows.\n\nPair 3 checks the case where a line does not reach a seed column. Lines at rows 3 and 8 never reach column 9, so column 9 only changes colour at rows 2, 5 and 11, which matches the output.\n\n**The rule as a procedure.**\n1. Find the gap columns: the nonzero cells in the bottom row.\n2. For each such column x, scan downward over the background cells.\n3. Give each empty cell (y, x) the colour of the first row y′ ≥ y where something is present: a horizontal-line neighbour at x±1, or a nonzero cell at x itself (the seed).\n\n**Applying it to the test.**\n- Column 3, seed 6:\n  - rows 0–2 become 2 (the 2-line at row 2);\n  - rows 3–6 become 3 (the 3-line at row 6);\n  - rows 7–11 become 4 (the 4-line at row 11);\n  - row 12 becomes 6, the seed colour.\n- Column 8, seed 8:\n  - rows 0–3 become 5;\n  - rows 4–8 become 9;\n  - rows 9–11 become 4;\n  - rows 12–13 become 8.\n\nThe program emits one teaching stage per filled column.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    cols = [x for x in range(W) if g[H-1, x] != bg]\n    for x in cols:\n        for y in range(H):\n            if g[y, x] != bg:\n                continue\n            colour = None\n            for yy in range(y, H):\n                if g[yy, x] != bg:\n                    colour = g[yy, x]; break\n                if x - 1 >= 0 and g[yy, x-1] != bg:\n                    colour = g[yy, x-1]; break\n                if x + 1 < W and g[yy, x+1] != bg:\n                    colour = g[yy, x+1]; break\n            if colour is not None:\n                g[y, x] = colour\n        emit(g, f\"Fill gap column {x} top to bottom: each segment takes the colour of the next horizontal line below it, and the stretch below the last line takes the seed colour.\")\n    emit(g, \"All gap columns filled; final output.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"68fae9d187ddbf47b342112a856377c06bdb8c70f2c704dc12ba7aeb7c26db32","task_sha256":"3b436d85d4e891c8ff6385af3a54fe061c2a1c50cf954814bc185a0650112b5c","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
